---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="Called the People? — Daf Yomi Game: Chullin 131"
  description="Chullin 131: a Levite snatches gifts meant for the kohen, and Rav can't decide whether to make him pay — is a Levite called 'the people' who owe the foreleg, jaw, and maw? Work the gift docket on nine real cases, then follow four failed proofs and one conclusive refutation before Mareimar closes the file for good."
>
  <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com" />
  <link
    rel="stylesheet"
    href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Spectral:wght@500;600;700;800&family=Frank+Ruhl+Libre:wght@400;700;900&display=swap"
  />

  <main class="min-h-screen bg-[#0c0905] !text-[#ecdfc4]">
    <!-- ============ Hero ============ -->
    <div class="border-b border-[#33230f] bg-gradient-to-b from-[#1a1108] to-transparent px-4 pt-10 pb-8 text-center">
      <p class="mb-3 text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.3em] !text-[#c99a4a]">
        <a href="/daf" class="hover:!text-[#e8c877]">daf yomi arcade</a> · chullin 131 · 2026-09-22
      </p>
      <h1 class="spectral text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight sm:text-5xl">🌾 Called the People?</h1>
      <p class="frank mt-3 text-2xl font-bold !text-[#e8c877]" dir="rtl" lang="he">אִי אִיקְּרוֹ ״עַם״</p>
      <p class="mx-auto mt-1 text-xs italic !text-[#9d8f70]">
        "If they are called 'the people'..." — Rav's unresolved question about Levites, Chullin 131a:9
      </p>
      <p class="mx-auto mt-4 max-w-xl text-sm leading-relaxed !text-[#c7b899]">
        A Levite has been snatching gifts of the priesthood out of children's hands on their way to the
        kohen. Rav rebukes him — but won't force him to hand over his <i>own</i> foreleg, jaw, and maw. Why
        the difference? Because Rav genuinely doesn't know whether a Levite counts as "the people" the
        Torah obligates. Every case on this daf runs through one question: does beit din reach into a hand
        and take it out, or does the hand keep what it holds? Work the docket.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="mx-auto max-w-2xl px-4 pb-16 pt-8">
      <!-- ============ Context cards ============ -->
      <div class="mb-8 rounded-2xl border border-[#33230f] bg-[#18110a] p-5">
        <p class="text-[11px] font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] !text-[#c99a4a]">before you play</p>
        <div class="mt-3 grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-2">
          <div class="rounded-xl border border-[#2a1d0e] bg-[#100b06] p-3">
            <p class="text-xs font-bold !text-[#e8c877]">🥩 the exemption underneath</p>
            <p class="mt-1 text-xs leading-relaxed !text-[#a99878]">
              Rav Chisda: one who damages or eats gifts of the priesthood pays nothing — but only if they
              were never a distinct, separated object to begin with (<a class="!text-[#e8c877] underline hover:!text-[#f3dba0]" href="https://www.sefaria.org/Chullin.131a.1" target="_blank" rel="noopener">131a:1</a>).
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="rounded-xl border border-[#2a1d0e] bg-[#100b06] p-3">
            <p class="text-xs font-bold !text-[#e8c877]">🐑 the snatching Levite</p>
            <p class="mt-1 text-xs leading-relaxed !text-[#a99878]">
              A certain Levite grabbed gifts from children delivering them for their fathers. Rav: isn't it
              enough that we don't take gifts from him — must he also snatch? (<a class="!text-[#e8c877] underline hover:!text-[#f3dba0]" href="https://www.sefaria.org/Chullin.131a.8" target="_blank" rel="noopener">131a:8</a>)
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="rounded-xl border border-[#2a1d0e] bg-[#100b06] p-3">
            <p class="text-xs font-bold !text-[#e8c877]">❓ the unresolved doubt</p>
            <p class="mt-1 text-xs leading-relaxed !text-[#a99878]">
              Are Levites called "the people" who owe the foreleg, jaw, and maw (Deuteronomy 18:3)? Rav
              never decides — he is simply, genuinely unsure (<a class="!text-[#e8c877] underline hover:!text-[#f3dba0]" href="https://www.sefaria.org/Chullin.131a.9" target="_blank" rel="noopener">131a:9–10</a>).
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="rounded-xl border border-[#2a1d0e] bg-[#100b06] p-3">
            <p class="text-xs font-bold !text-[#e8c877]">📜 the final word</p>
            <p class="mt-1 text-xs leading-relaxed !text-[#a99878]">
              Mareimar: the halakha follows Rav (the doubt stands, so beit din does not compel) — and the
              halakha follows Rav Chisda (no payment for what was never distinct) (<a class="!text-[#e8c877] underline hover:!text-[#f3dba0]" href="https://www.sefaria.org/Chullin.131b.20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">131b:20</a>).
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- ============ Score strip ============ -->
      <div class="sticky top-2 z-20 mb-8 flex flex-wrap items-center justify-center gap-x-5 gap-y-1 rounded-2xl border border-[#33230f] bg-[#100b06]/95 px-4 py-2.5 text-center shadow-lg shadow-black/40 backdrop-blur">
        <span class="text-[11px] uppercase tracking-[0.2em] !text-[#a99878]">the docket</span>
        <span id="score-docket" class="spectral text-base font-bold !text-[#e8c877]">0 / 9</span>
        <span class="text-[11px] uppercase tracking-[0.2em] !text-[#a99878]">the doubt file</span>
        <span id="score-doubt" class="spectral text-base font-bold !text-[#e8c877]">0 / 4</span>
      </div>

      <!-- ============ Act I: The Gift Docket ============ -->
      <section aria-labelledby="act1-h">
        <p class="text-[11px] font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.25em] !text-[#c99a4a]">act i · the gift docket</p>
        <h2 id="act1-h" class="spectral mt-1 text-2xl font-bold !text-[#ecdfc4]">Whose hand does it stay in?</h2>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed !text-[#c7b899]">
          Nine real cases from the daf. For each, rule one thing: does beit din reach into the current
          holder's hand and remove the gift, does it stay put, or was there never a distinct object to
          remove in the first place?
        </p>
        <div class="mt-4 grid grid-cols-3 gap-2 text-center text-[11px] font-bold uppercase tracking-wide">
          <div class="rounded-lg border border-[#c99a4a]/40 bg-[#c99a4a]/10 px-2 py-2 !text-[#e8c877]">מוֹצִיאִין<br /><span class="font-normal normal-case tracking-normal">beit din takes it</span></div>
          <div class="rounded-lg border border-[#8b93ab]/40 bg-[#8b93ab]/10 px-2 py-2 !text-[#b7bfd6]">אֵין מוֹצִיאִין<br /><span class="font-normal normal-case tracking-normal">it stays put</span></div>
          <div class="rounded-lg border border-[#b3705a]/40 bg-[#b3705a]/10 px-2 py-2 !text-[#d99a83]">פָּטוּר<br /><span class="font-normal normal-case tracking-normal">nothing to take</span></div>
        </div>
        <div id="docket" class="mt-5 space-y-4"></div>
      </section>

      <!-- ============ Act II: The Doubt File ============ -->
      <section aria-labelledby="act2-h" class="mt-14">
        <p class="text-[11px] font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.25em] !text-[#c99a4a]">act ii · the doubt file</p>
        <h2 id="act2-h" class="spectral mt-1 text-2xl font-bold !text-[#ecdfc4]">Four attempts to settle it</h2>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed !text-[#c7b899]">
          Four times the Gemara says "Come and hear!" — bringing a baraita that looks like it settles
          whether Levites are "the people." Every time, Rav's doubt survives, except once, when it almost
          doesn't. Follow the real sequence.
        </p>
        <div id="doubt-file" class="mt-5 space-y-4"></div>
        <div id="mareimar" class="mt-4 hidden rounded-2xl border border-[#e8c877]/40 bg-[#241a0a] p-5 text-center"></div>
      </section>

      <!-- ============ Act III: The Cliffhanger ============ -->
      <section aria-labelledby="act3-h" class="mt-14">
        <p class="text-[11px] font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.25em] !text-[#c99a4a]">act iii · one last case</p>
        <h2 id="act3-h" class="spectral mt-1 text-2xl font-bold !text-[#ecdfc4]">Ulla and the kohenet</h2>
        <div id="coda" class="mt-4"></div>
      </section>

      <!-- ============ Learn the real daf ============ -->
      <div class="mt-14 rounded-2xl border border-[#33230f] bg-[#18110a] p-5">
        <p class="text-[11px] font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] !text-[#c99a4a]">learn the real daf</p>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed !text-[#c7b899]">
          Chullin 131 runs from Rav Chisda's exemption through the snatching Levite's story, the poor
          person's four/three/two field gifts and their verses, the doubt over Levites and "the people,"
          and Mareimar's closing ruling — before turning, mid-sentence, to Ulla and the kohenet.
        </p>
        <div class="mt-4 flex flex-wrap gap-2">
          <a href="https://www.sefaria.org/Chullin.131a" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="rounded-lg border border-[#e8c877]/40 bg-[#e8c877]/10 px-3 py-1.5 text-xs font-bold !text-[#e8c877] hover:bg-[#e8c877]/20">Chullin 131 on Sefaria →</a>
          <a href="https://www.sefaria.org/Rashi_on_Chullin.131a" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="rounded-lg border border-[#2a1d0e] px-3 py-1.5 text-xs !text-[#a99878] hover:!text-[#ecdfc4]">Rashi (×67)</a>
          <a href="https://www.sefaria.org/Steinsaltz_on_Chullin.131a" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="rounded-lg border border-[#2a1d0e] px-3 py-1.5 text-xs !text-[#a99878] hover:!text-[#ecdfc4]">Steinsaltz (×39)</a>
          <a href="https://www.sefaria.org/Sefer_Mitzvot_Gadol_on_Chullin.131a" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="rounded-lg border border-[#2a1d0e] px-3 py-1.5 text-xs !text-[#a99878] hover:!text-[#ecdfc4]">Sefer Mitzvot Gadol (×10)</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </main>

  <style>
    .spectral { font-family: 'Spectral', 'Times New Roman', serif; }
    .frank { font-family: 'Frank Ruhl Libre', 'Times New Roman', serif; }

    .verdict-btn {
      border: 1px solid #2a1d0e;
      background: #100b06;
      color: #ecdfc4;
      font-size: 12px;
      font-weight: 700;
      border-radius: 10px;
      padding: 10px 8px;
      cursor: pointer;
      line-height: 1.25;
    }
    .verdict-btn:hover { border-color: #e8c877; color: #f3dba0; }
    .verdict-btn:disabled { cursor: default; }

    .case-card, .doubt-card {
      border: 1px solid #33230f;
      background: #18110a;
      border-radius: 14px;
      padding: 16px;
    }
    .case-card.solved-take { border-color: #c99a4a80; }
    .case-card.solved-stay { border-color: #8b93ab80; }
    .case-card.solved-gone { border-color: #b3705a80; }

    .reveal { border: 1px solid #2a1d0e; background: #100b06; border-radius: 10px; padding: 10px 12px; }

    @keyframes shake {
      10%, 90% { transform: translateX(-1px); }
      20%, 80% { transform: translateX(2px); }
      30%, 50%, 70% { transform: translateX(-4px); }
      40%, 60% { transform: translateX(4px); }
    }
    .shake { animation: shake 0.4s; }
    @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
      .shake { animation: none; }
    }
  </style>

  <script>
    const sef = (ref: string, label: string) =>
      `<a class="!text-[#e8c877] underline hover:!text-[#f3dba0]" href="https://www.sefaria.org/${ref}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">${label}</a>`;

    // =====================================================================
    // Act I — The Gift Docket
    // =====================================================================
    type Bin = 'take' | 'stay' | 'gone';
    const BIN_LABEL: Record<Bin, string> = {
      take: 'מוֹצִיאִין — beit din takes it',
      stay: 'אֵין מוֹצִיאִין — it stays put',
      gone: 'פָּטוּר — nothing to take',
    };
    const BIN_COLOR: Record<Bin, string> = { take: '#e8c877', stay: '#b7bfd6', gone: '#d99a83' };

    const CASES: { title: string; text: string; bin: Bin; explain: string }[] = [
      {
        title: 'An untithed storeroom',
        text: "A Levite's grain sits untithed at home — no teruma, terumat maaser, or challah has been separated out yet.",
        bin: 'take',
        explain: `Anything <b>of sanctity</b> — teruma, terumat maaser, challah — beit din reaches in and
          takes it, from anyone's hand, Levite included. This is the principle: ${sef('Chullin.131b.12', '131b:12')}.`,
      },
      {
        title: 'The foreleg on the shelf',
        text: 'A Levite slaughtered his own animal. The foreleg, the jaw, and the maw sit in his house, never sent to any kohen.',
        bin: 'stay',
        explain: `This is the daf's central doubt: is a Levite called "the people" who owe these three
          gifts (Deuteronomy 18:3)? Rav could never decide, and Mareimar later rules that when the law
          stays this unsettled, the hand already holding it wins — beit din does not reach in.
          ${sef('Chullin.131a.9', '131a:9–10')}, ${sef('Chullin.131b.20', '131b:20')}.`,
      },
      {
        title: "One kohen's portion, another kohen's claim",
        text: "The foreleg and jaw already reached a kohen's kitchen. A second kohen shows up wanting a share.",
        bin: 'stay',
        explain: `"Neither from a kohen to a kohen, nor from a Levite to a Levite" — once ordinary
          (non-sanctified) gifts land in a hand that legitimately qualifies for them, they stay there,
          even against an equally qualified claimant. ${sef('Chullin.131a.13', '131a:13')}, ${sef('Chullin.131b.7', '131b:7')}.`,
      },
      {
        title: 'The vineyard owner who wants it all',
        text: 'A poor man owns his own small vineyard. At harvest he eyes the dropped grapes and the forgotten clusters and keeps them for his own family, instead of leaving them for the rest of the poor.',
        bin: 'take',
        explain: `"Azivah" — leaving — is written of peret, olelot, shikcha, and pe'ah (Leviticus 19:10,
          23:22; Deuteronomy 24:19–21). Even a poor person who owns the field must leave them for all the
          poor; if he won't, beit din removes them from his own hand.
          ${sef('Chullin.131a.12', '131a:12')}, ${sef('Chullin.131b.3', '131b:3–4')}.`,
      },
      {
        title: 'Maaser ani kept at home',
        text: "This year's poor man's tithe sits undistributed in the owner's house.",
        bin: 'take',
        explain: `Here the verse says "netina" — giving (Deuteronomy 26:12) — so the owner keeps the
          benefit of discretion, choosing which poor person receives it. But if he refuses to give it at
          all, beit din still removes it from his hand. ${sef('Chullin.131a.13', '131a:13')}, ${sef('Chullin.131b.5', '131b:5–6')}.`,
      },
      {
        title: 'The maw sold whole',
        text: 'A butcher sells "the innards of this cow" as one set. Tucked inside, still unseparated, is the maw owed to the kohen. The butcher wants its value left out of the sale entirely.',
        bin: 'take',
        explain: `The gift is still "in their own right" — a whole, distinct object — so the buyer must
          hand it to the kohen, and the butcher may <i>not</i> deduct its worth from the price; it was
          never really sold to him. ${sef('Chullin.131a.4', '131a:4–5')}.`,
      },
      {
        title: 'The same maw, sold by the pound',
        text: "This time the Israelite bought the innards by weight — the maw's value folded openly into the price.",
        bin: 'take',
        explain: `Still handed to the kohen — but now the butcher <i>does</i> deduct its value, since
          selling by weight means both sides knowingly priced it in. ${sef('Chullin.131a.4', '131a:4')}.`,
      },
      {
        title: "First-shearing wool, still on the floor",
        text: "The first fleece off a Levite's own flock hasn't yet gone to any kohen.",
        bin: 'take',
        explain: `The verse for reishit hagez (Deuteronomy 18:4) doesn't limit it to "the people" the way
          the foreleg-jaw-maw verse does — so even a Levite owes it, and beit din takes it straight from
          his hand. ${sef('Chullin.131b.11', '131b:11')}.`,
      },
      {
        title: 'The stew nobody sifted',
        text: 'A pot of grain was cooked and eaten before anyone pulled its unseparated priestly portion out. There was never a moment when that portion existed as its own separate thing.',
        bin: 'gone',
        explain: `Rav Chisda's rule underneath the whole daf: you can't be made to pay for a gift that was
          never a distinct, separated object when it was consumed. There's nothing to repossess, because
          there was never a "thing" to hold — contrast the intact maw above.
          ${sef('Chullin.131a.1', '131a:1')}, ${sef('Chullin.131a.5', '131a:5')}.`,
      },
    ];

    let docketScore = 0;
    const docketEl = document.getElementById('docket')!;
    const scoreDocketEl = document.getElementById('score-docket')!;

    CASES.forEach((c) => {
      const card = document.createElement('div');
      card.className = 'case-card';
      card.innerHTML = `
        <p class="spectral text-base font-bold !text-[#ecdfc4]">${c.title}</p>
        <p class="mt-1.5 text-sm leading-relaxed !text-[#c7b899]">${c.text}</p>
        <div class="mt-3 grid grid-cols-3 gap-2"></div>
        <div class="reveal mt-3 hidden text-sm leading-relaxed !text-[#c7b899]" aria-live="polite"></div>
      `;
      const grid = card.querySelector('.grid')!;
      const reveal = card.querySelector('.reveal')! as HTMLElement;
      let firstTry = true;
      let settled = false;

      (Object.keys(BIN_LABEL) as Bin[]).forEach((bin) => {
        const btn = document.createElement('button');
        btn.className = 'verdict-btn';
        btn.innerHTML = BIN_LABEL[bin];
        btn.addEventListener('click', () => {
          if (settled) return;
          if (bin === c.bin) {
            settled = true;
            card.classList.add(`solved-${bin}`);
            reveal.classList.remove('hidden');
            reveal.innerHTML = `<b style="color:${BIN_COLOR[bin]}">${BIN_LABEL[bin].split(' — ')[0]}.</b> ${c.explain}`;
            grid.querySelectorAll('button').forEach((b) => {
              if (b !== btn) (b as HTMLButtonElement).style.opacity = '0.35';
              (b as HTMLButtonElement).disabled = true;
            });
            if (firstTry) {
              docketScore++;
              scoreDocketEl.textContent = `${docketScore} / ${CASES.length}`;
            }
          } else {
            firstTry = false;
            card.classList.remove('shake');
            void (card as HTMLElement).offsetWidth;
            card.classList.add('shake');
            reveal.classList.remove('hidden');
            reveal.innerHTML = `<span style="color:#d99a83">Not quite.</span> Think about whether this gift is sanctified, still intact, or already gone — then try another bin.`;
          }
        });
        grid.appendChild(btn);
      });
      docketEl.appendChild(card);
    });

    // =====================================================================
    // Act II — The Doubt File
    // =====================================================================
    type Round = {
      label: string;
      text: string;
      question: string;
      options: { text: string; ok: boolean }[];
      explain: string;
    };

    const ROUNDS: Round[] = [
      {
        label: 'round 1 · property of a kohen',
        text: `A baraita lists nine things "belonging to a kohen," and elsewhere states: these ordinary
          gifts move "neither from kohen to kohen, nor from Levite to Levite" — implying that from
          <i>Levite to kohen</i>, they ARE removed.`,
        question: 'That would mean Levites are called "the people." How does Rav\'s position survive?',
        options: [
          { text: 'The baraita never means the actual foreleg, jaw, and maw — it means something merely like them: first tithe.', ok: true },
          { text: 'The baraita is talking about a different category of Levite entirely.', ok: false },
          { text: 'Removing gifts from a Levite to a kohen never actually happens; the baraita was misquoted.', ok: false },
        ],
        explain: `"Like the foreleg — but not the foreleg" turns out to mean first tithe (later refined
          further, to first-shorn wool). The real zeroa, lechayayim, and keva are never addressed.
          ${sef('Chullin.131b.7', '131b:7')}, ${sef('Chullin.131b.11', '131b:8–11')}.`,
      },
      {
        label: 'round 2 · sanctity flips it',
        text: `A different baraita states the principle directly: anything <i>of sanctity</i> is removed
          from a Levite's hand; anything <i>not</i> of sanctity — such as the foreleg, jaw, and maw — is
          not. That implies Levites are NOT called "the people."`,
        question: 'This baraita argues the opposite conclusion from round 1. Now what?',
        options: [
          { text: 'Same dodge as before — here too, "foreleg, jaw, and maw" really means first tithe (after Ezra\'s penalty), so this baraita never touches the real gifts either.', ok: true },
          { text: 'Sanctified and non-sanctified gifts simply follow unrelated rules, so there is no real contradiction to resolve.', ok: false },
          { text: 'This baraita is actually describing kohanim, not Levites, so it says nothing relevant.', ok: false },
        ],
        explain: `Once again, the phrase is read as "like the foreleg, but not the foreleg" — meaning
          first tithe, specifically after Ezra penalized the Levites and reassigned it to the kohanim.
          ${sef('Chullin.131b.12', '131b:12–13')}.`,
      },
      {
        label: 'round 3 · who did you slaughter for?',
        text: `A baraita: "One who slaughters for a kohen or a gentile is exempt from giving the gifts."
          The Gemara first narrows the implied flip side down to just "for an Israelite" — safe for Rav.
          But then a second, blunter baraita turns up: "for a kohen or a gentile — exempt; for a
          <b>Levite</b> or an Israelite — obligated." Explicit. Unavoidable.`,
        question: 'What does the Gemara call this second baraita?',
        options: [
          { text: 'תְּיוּבְתָּא — a conclusive refutation of Rav. For once, there is no dodge available.', ok: true },
          { text: 'Another case where "Levite" secretly means first tithe.', ok: false },
          { text: 'A scribal error that later authorities emend away.', ok: false },
        ],
        explain: `For a moment, the sugya genuinely defeats Rav — the daf itself uses the word
          תְּיוּבְתָּא, "a refutation." ${sef('Chullin.131b.14', '131b:14–15')}.`,
      },
      {
        label: "round 4 · Rav's last move",
        text: `Rav has one move left: claim this is a dispute among the tanna'im themselves. Two baraitot
          read the same Yom Kippur atonement verse (Leviticus 16:33) differently. One assigns its extra
          "atonement" clause to the Levites — meaning they needed a separate mention, since "the people of
          the assembly" didn't already cover them. The other assigns that same clause to Canaanite slaves
          instead — meaning the Levites were already covered by "the people," freeing the clause up for
          slaves.`,
        question: 'So which tanna is right?',
        options: [
          { text: "It's a genuine, live dispute — and Rav himself isn't sure which side is halakha. The doubt survives by becoming a doubt about a doubt.", ok: true },
        ],
        explain: `${sef('Chullin.131b.16', '131b:16–19')}. Asked why Rav doesn't just pick a side, the
          Gemara answers: "מְסַפְּקָא לֵיהּ אִי כְּהַאי תַּנָּא אִי כְּהַאי תַּנָּא" — he is uncertain whether the
          halakha follows this tanna or that one.`,
      },
    ];

    let doubtScore = 0;
    const doubtEl = document.getElementById('doubt-file')!;
    const scoreDoubtEl = document.getElementById('score-doubt')!;
    let doubtAnswered = 0;

    ROUNDS.forEach((r) => {
      const card = document.createElement('div');
      card.className = 'doubt-card';
      card.innerHTML = `
        <p class="text-[11px] font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] !text-[#c99a4a]">${r.label}</p>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed !text-[#c7b899]">${r.text}</p>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm font-bold !text-[#ecdfc4]">${r.question}</p>
        <div class="mt-3 space-y-2"></div>
        <div class="reveal mt-3 hidden text-sm leading-relaxed !text-[#c7b899]" aria-live="polite"></div>
      `;
      const opts = card.querySelector('.space-y-2')!;
      const reveal = card.querySelector('.reveal')! as HTMLElement;
      let firstTry = true;
      let settled = false;

      r.options.forEach((o) => {
        const btn = document.createElement('button');
        btn.className = 'verdict-btn w-full text-left';
        btn.textContent = o.text;
        btn.addEventListener('click', () => {
          if (settled) return;
          if (o.ok) {
            settled = true;
            reveal.classList.remove('hidden');
            reveal.innerHTML = `<b style="color:#e8c877">Right.</b> ${r.explain}`;
            opts.querySelectorAll('button').forEach((b) => {
              if (b !== btn) (b as HTMLButtonElement).style.opacity = '0.35';
              (b as HTMLButtonElement).disabled = true;
            });
            if (firstTry) {
              doubtScore++;
              scoreDoubtEl.textContent = `${doubtScore} / ${ROUNDS.length}`;
            }
            doubtAnswered++;
            maybeMareimar();
          } else {
            firstTry = false;
            card.classList.remove('shake');
            void (card as HTMLElement).offsetWidth;
            card.classList.add('shake');
            reveal.classList.remove('hidden');
            reveal.innerHTML = `<span style="color:#d99a83">Not the real dodge.</span> Try again.`;
          }
        });
        opts.appendChild(btn);
      });
      doubtEl.appendChild(card);
    });

    function maybeMareimar() {
      if (doubtAnswered < ROUNDS.length) return;
      const m = document.getElementById('mareimar')!;
      m.classList.remove('hidden');
      m.innerHTML = `
        <p class="text-[11px] uppercase tracking-[0.25em] !text-[#c99a4a]">the file closes</p>
        <p class="frank mt-2 text-xl font-bold !text-[#e8c877]" dir="rtl" lang="he">הִלְכְתָא כְּוָותֵיהּ דְּרַב, וְהִלְכְתָא כְּוָותֵיהּ דְּרַב חִסְדָּא</p>
        <p class="mx-auto mt-3 max-w-md text-sm leading-relaxed !text-[#c7b899]">
          Mareimar taught: the halakha follows <b>Rav</b> — the doubt stands, so beit din does not compel a
          Levite to give his own foreleg, jaw, and maw — and the halakha follows <b>Rav Chisda</b> — no
          payment is owed for a gift that was never a distinct object when it was consumed or damaged.
          Four proofs rose against the doubt. All four fell — one only barely. The uncertainty itself
          becomes the law. ${sef('Chullin.131b.20', '131b:20')}
        </p>`;
      const reduce = window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches;
      m.scrollIntoView({ behavior: reduce ? 'auto' : 'smooth', block: 'center' });
    }

    // =====================================================================
    // Act III — The cliffhanger
    // =====================================================================
    const codaEl = document.getElementById('coda')!;
    const codaCard = document.createElement('div');
    codaCard.className = 'doubt-card cursor-pointer';
    codaCard.innerHTML = `
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed !text-[#c7b899]">
        Ulla had a practice: he would give gifts of the priesthood to a <b>kohenet</b> — a priest's
        daughter — even one married to an Israelite. Rava challenges him: the leftover of a kohenet's own
        grain offering may be eaten, like an Israelite's — but the leftover of a <i>kohen's</i> own grain
        offering may not (Leviticus 6:16). If "kohen" in that verse already included "kohenet," how could
        her leftovers be eaten at all?
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.2em] !text-[#c99a4a]">tap for Ulla's answer</p>
      <div class="reveal mt-3 hidden text-sm leading-relaxed !text-[#c7b899]"></div>
    `;
    let codaOpen = false;
    codaCard.addEventListener('click', () => {
      if (codaOpen) return;
      codaOpen = true;
      const reveal = codaCard.querySelector('.reveal')! as HTMLElement;
      reveal.classList.remove('hidden');
      reveal.innerHTML = `<span class="frank" dir="rtl" lang="he">אֲמַר לֵיהּ: רַבִּי,</span> — "Ulla said to
        him: My teacher, ..." and there the daf stops. Chullin 131 ends mid-sentence.
        ${sef('Chullin.131b.21', '131b:21–22')} — the answer waits on 132.`;
    });
    codaEl.appendChild(codaCard);
  </script>
</Layout>
